So the issue is that after typing the date, the onChange handler triggers getAvailableHours and updateConfirmFrame. However, there is also an onMonthChange handler that runs after a timeout (500ms) and calls getUnavailableDates, which then applies unavailable dates and possibly sets the date to the first available date (the loop in applyUnavailableDates). That overrides the user's typed date.

Specifically, in applyUnavailableDates, there is a block:

if (setDate && !vars('manage_mode')) {
    for (let i = 1; i <= numberOfDays; i++) {
        const currentDate = new Date(selectedDate.getFullYear(), selectedDate.getMonth(), i);
        if (unavailableDates.indexOf(moment(currentDate).format('YYYY-MM-DD')) === -1) {
            App.Utils.UI.setDateTimePickerValue($selectDate, currentDate);
            getAvailableHours(moment(currentDate).format('YYYY-MM-DD'));
            break;
        }
    }
}

This sets the date to the first available day in the month (starting from day 1). Since the user typed 02/11/2026 (which is the 2nd), but the first day (01/11/2026) is available, it gets set to the 1st.

Why does setDate become true? Looking at the call chain: onMonthChange calls getUnavailableDates with setDate=true (the third argument). Indeed, in initializeDatePicker, the onMonthChange callback passes setDate as true? Let's check:

